steps.body:
A memo works top down: you ask for the big answer and recursion fills in the small ones. A **table** works bottom up: fill `dp[0]`, then `dp[1]`, and so on, so every entry you need is already there when you get to it. This is dynamic programming, and with no recursion there is no call stack to overflow.

Build a table in three moves: say what one entry means, write the rule from earlier entries, and fill the first entries by hand.

```javascript
const n = 6;
const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); // dp[i]: ways to climb i stairs
dp[0] = 1;
dp[1] = 1;
for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
  dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2];
}
console.log(dp.join(" ")); // 1 1 2 3 5 8 13
```

That is the climbing stairs count from the memo part, without a single recursive call. For a best answer instead of a count, the rule picks between choices with `Math.min` or `Math.max`. For a minimum, start empty entries at `Infinity` so any real cost beats them.

When each entry needs only the last two, two variables can replace the array, but the table is easier to print and check while you are learning.

**The gotcha:** `new Array(n)` has n slots, indexes 0 to n - 1. A table that needs `dp[n]` must be made with `new Array(n + 1)`.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const cost = input.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);
const n = cost.length;

// dp[i]: the cheapest total to stand on step i
const dp = new Array(n).fill(0);
dp[0] = cost[0];
if (n > 1) {
  dp[1] = cost[1];
}
for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
  dp[i] = cost[i] + Math.min(dp[i - 1], dp[i - 2]);
}

const cheapest = n > 1 ? Math.min(dp[n - 1], dp[n - 2]) : dp[0];
console.log(`table: ${dp.join(" ")}`);
console.log(`cheapest: ${cheapest}`);

